window.ESSAY_COPY.en = {
  "doc.title": "A Secret Agreed in Public · From Number Theory to Post-Quantum Cryptography",
  "doc.desc": "How can two strangers agree on a secret while everyone listens? One-way functions, Diffie–Hellman, RSA, elliptic curves, certificate chains, the quantum threat and lattice cryptography, each step computed with real arithmetic in the page.",
  "nav.back": "← Knowledge Index",
  "nav.lang": "Language",
  "toc.label": "Contents",
  "toc.title": "Contents",
  "u.long": "{head}… ({n} digits)",
  "u.ym": "{mon} {y}",
  "u.asof": "{d}",

  "hero.kicker": "Computing & AI · Systems & Security · From First Principles to the Frontier",
  "hero.title": "A Secret Agreed in Public",
  "hero.sub": "From clock arithmetic to post-quantum cryptography:<br>one-way functions, signatures, certificates, quantum computers and lattices",
  "hero.lead": "Every time you open an https web page, your browser and a server you have never met agree — over a line anyone can tap — on a number only the two of them know, then encrypt everything that follows with it. This essay asks one question: <strong>how can two strangers agree on a secret while everyone is listening?</strong> The answer climbs a ladder from number theory to post-quantum cryptography, with a live model at every rung: one-way functions, Diffie–Hellman, RSA, elliptic curves, signatures and certificates, bits of security, the quantum threat, lattices and learning with errors. Chapter 10 records where the field stands as of November 2026.",
  "hero.note": "This page was written by an AI (Claude). Every chapter runs real arithmetic in your browser (using JavaScript BigInts), only on small numbers; each chapter ends by stating how much larger real parameters are. The frontier facts in Chapter 10 carry dates and sources, and they will go stale.",
  "hero.warn": "<strong>Safety notice:</strong> The cryptographic operations on this page are computed for real, but with parameters small enough to break in seconds, no padding, predictable random numbers, and no protection against timing attacks. They are here only to show how the ideas work and must never be used to protect anything.",

  /* ───── 01 ───── */
  "ch1.title": "The Key Cannot Be Sent",
  "ch1.law": "The maths of encryption came early; the hard part is that two people need the same key first, and the key cannot travel over the tapped line.",
  "ch1.p1": "Suppose you want to mail a locked box to a friend. The lock is strong, so how do you get him the key? If you send the key by courier too, anyone who can peek at the box can peek at the key. From ancient substitution ciphers to modern AES, almost all encryption is \"symmetric\": the same key locks and unlocks. Symmetric ciphers such as AES are already formidable; the problem does not lie there.",
  "ch1.p2": "The problem lies in distributing the key. The old answer was to meet beforehand or send a codebook by trusted courier. Once many people are involved, that breaks down: every pair needs a key shared only by those two.",
  "ch1.model.title": "One key for every pair of people",
  "ch1.model.how": "Drag the number of people to see how many lines connect every pair (each line is a shared key that must be delivered safely beforehand). Then switch to \"Public keys\" for a different arrangement.",
  "ch1.model.aria": "Pairwise shared keys required as the number of people grows",
  "ch1.leg.pair": "One shared key delivered beforehand",
  "ch1.leg.person": "One person",
  "ch1.ctl.n": "People",
  "ch1.ctl.mode": "Key arrangement",
  "ch1.mode.sym": "Pairwise shared keys",
  "ch1.mode.pub": "Public keys",
  "ch1.read.pairs": "Pairwise keys needed",
  "ch1.read.pub": "Key pairs under public-key scheme",
  "ch1.read.join": "New keys if one more person joins",
  "ch1.read.big": "Pairwise keys for 100,000 people",
  "ch1.unit.people": "{v} people",
  "ch1.say.sym": "For {n} people to talk pairwise, <em>{pairs}</em> distinct keys must be delivered in advance; add one more person and {join} new keys must go out — every one of them over an untapped channel.",
  "ch1.say.pub": "With public keys, each person generates one pair and pins the public half on a notice board: {n} people need {n} pairs in total, and a newcomer simply posts their own. The remaining puzzle is how to build a secret known only to two people out of purely public data — that is what the next chapters solve.",
  "ch1.p3": "The count is exact: pairing n people gives the combination n(n−1)/2. A city of 100,000 people needs 4,999,950,000 keys. In 1976, Whitfield Diffie and Martin Hellman published \"New Directions in Cryptography\", asking what sounded impossible at the time: can two people compute a shared key on the spot over a completely public wire? Everything that follows explains how that works, what holds it up, and what can break it.",
  "ch1.p4": "First, set the goal clearly: every bit sent across the line can be read by onlookers, and we are not even sure who is at the other end (Chapter 3 shows why that matters). We want to produce a number known only to the two participants and use it as the key for a symmetric cipher. Public-key cryptography only handles key agreement and signatures; bulk data is still encrypted by much faster symmetric ciphers.",
  "ch1.limit": "This counter only tracks the number of keys, ignoring the trouble of storing, rotating, and revoking them; in practice those are just as hard, which is what Chapter 6's certificates are built for.",

  /* ───── 02 ───── */
  "ch2.title": "A One-Way Door: Easy Forward, Hard Backwards",
  "ch2.law": "All of public-key cryptography rests on operations that are fast to compute forward and painfully slow to invert.",
  "ch2.p1": "Stirring two paints together is easy; separating the mixed colour back into the original two is nearly impossible. One of mathematics' favourite doors of this kind is modular exponentiation: pick a prime p and a base g, and take the remainder of g to the power x divided by p.",
  "ch2.eq1": "y = g<sup>x</sup> mod p",
  "ch2.p2": "Mod p is clock arithmetic, keeping only the remainder after dividing by p (the \"Clock Arithmetic\" step in <a href=\"../rsa-encryption/\">RSA Public-Key Encryption · Easy to Multiply, Hard to Split</a> covers it in full). Forward: given g, x, and p, find y. Backwards: given g, y, and p, find x — the <strong>discrete logarithm</strong>. First, see why the forward direction is so fast.",
  "ch2.modelA.title": "Square-and-multiply: computing 4ˣ mod 2039",
  "ch2.modelA.how": "Drag the exponent x. Write x in binary and read from the most significant bit downwards: square at every bit, and multiply by 4 whenever a bit is 1. Gold tiles mark the multiplications.",
  "ch2.ctl.x": "Exponent x",
  "ch2.bits": "x = {x} = binary <b>{b}</b>",
  "ch2.op.start": "Start: 4",
  "ch2.op.sq": "Bit {bit}: square",
  "ch2.op.mul": "Bit is 1: × 4",
  "ch2.read.sq": "Squarings",
  "ch2.read.mul": "Multiplications by 4",
  "ch2.read.val": "Result 4ˣ mod 2039",
  "ch2.read.naive": "Naive multiplications",
  "ch2.say.one": "At x = 1 nothing needs to be computed; the result is 4.",
  "ch2.say.trace": "{x} has {L} binary bits, so {sq} squarings and {mul} multiplications — <em>{tot} multiplications</em> in all — yield {v}. Multiplying {x} copies of 4 one by one would take {naive}. Reducing modulo {p} at every step keeps the numbers small throughout.",
  "ch2.p3": "That is \"square-and-multiply\". The number of multiplications depends only on the <strong>bit length</strong> of x: if x has k bits, it takes at most 2(k−1) multiplications. Even a 2048-bit exponent needs at most 4,094 multiplications — instant on any modern computer.",
  "ch2.p4": "What about going backwards — given y = 4<sup>x</sup> mod p, find x? The blunt approach tries them one by one: 4, 4², 4³… until hitting y. A smarter method is <strong>baby-step giant-step</strong> (Shanks, 1971): store the first m powers of 4 (baby steps), then jump backwards in strides of m (giant steps); setting m near √q guarantees the two meet within 2√q steps, where q is the number of distinct remainders powers of 4 take. Every p on this page is chosen as a \"safe prime\" p = 2q + 1 with q also prime, blocking shortcuts that exploit small factors of q (Pohlig–Hellman). Below, three algorithms race on the same instance.",
  "ch2.modelB.title": "The race: forward arithmetic vs two ways backwards",
  "ch2.modelB.how": "Drag the prime's bit length. Each change builds a fresh safe prime and secret x on the spot and runs the solvers for real. Cyan is forward multiplications, gold is the baby-step giant-step bound, and red is average brute-force steps; the vertical axis is log₂ of steps, so every 10 ticks is roughly 1,000× more work. White dots are measured runs; the red bar tracks live brute-force progress.",
  "ch2.modelB.aria": "Step counts of three algorithms versus prime bit length on a log₂ scale",
  "ch2.leg.fwd": "Forward (square-and-multiply)",
  "ch2.leg.bsgs": "Baby-step giant-step (bound 2√q)",
  "ch2.leg.brute": "Brute force (average q/2)",
  "ch2.leg.measured": "Measured",
  "ch2.ctl.bits": "Prime bit length",
  "ch2.btn.new": "New secret x",
  "ch2.read.fwd": "Forward: multiplications",
  "ch2.read.bsgs": "Baby-step giant-step: steps",
  "ch2.read.brute": "Brute force: steps",
  "ch2.read.eta": "Brute force: max time left at current rate",
  "ch2.cv.x": "Prime bit length →",
  "ch2.cv.y": "Steps (log₂)",
  "ch2.unit.bits": "{v} bits",
  "ch2.unit.sofar": "{v} tried…",
  "ch2.unit.found": "Found x",
  "ch2.unit.sec": "about {v} s",
  "ch2.unit.min": "about {v} min",
  "ch2.unit.hr": "about {v} hr",
  "ch2.unit.day": "about {v} days",
  "ch2.say.running": "{bits}-bit prime p = {p}. Forward took just <em>{fwd} multiplications</em>; baby-step giant-step took {bsgs} steps ({ms} ms), {ratioB}× forward; brute force has tried {brute} times and is still running (~{rate} million/s).",
  "ch2.say.done": "{bits} bits: forward took <em>{fwd}</em> multiplications; baby-step giant-step took {bsgs} steps; brute force needed {brute} steps to hit x — {ratio}× forward. Every +4 bits multiplies brute force by 16 on average, baby-step giant-step by 4, and leaves forward barely changed.",
  "ch2.say.reduce": "{bits} bits: forward took {fwd}; baby-step giant-step took {bsgs}. Brute force ran one short burst (animation off under reduced motion): {brute} steps at ~{rate} million/s, with its worst-case time remaining beside it.",
  "ch2.p5": "Each extra bit doubles average brute-force steps, multiplies baby-step giant-step by √2, and adds at most one or two multiplications forward. The gap keeps widening: at today's standard 2048 bits, brute force averages 2<sup>2046</sup> steps, baby-step giant-step still needs up to ~2<sup>1024.5</sup> steps, and forward stays at or below 4,094 multiplications.",
  "ch2.p6": "Even that is not the whole story. For discrete logs modulo p, there is a much faster attack than baby-step giant-step: the <strong>number field sieve</strong>, which exploits the fact that integers factor into small primes. Chapter 7 plots its cost; because of it, finite-field cryptography needs 2048-bit or larger p rather than a few hundred bits. Note too: nobody has proved that discrete logs are inherently hard — we only know that the best algorithms found over decades are still this slow.",
  "ch2.limit": "Primes here stop at 40 bits; real finite-field Diffie–Hellman groups start at 2048 bits (see RFC 7919 for standard TLS groups). Brute force runs only while this section is on screen, at a speed set by your device.",

  /* ───── 03 ───── */
  "ch3.title": "Diffie–Hellman: Agreeing on a Key in Public",
  "ch3.law": "Each person hides one number and sends only the forward result, yet both arrive at the same secret; an eavesdropper who wants it must invert.",
  "ch3.p1": "Back to paint: two people publicly pick a base colour; each mixes in a private colour and mails the mixture openly; then each mixes their own private colour into the jar they received. Both end up with the exact same blend (base plus both private colours), while anyone watching the road sees only the base and two partially mixed jars that must be unmixed to learn either secret. In numbers:",
  "ch3.eq1": "Alice: A = g<sup>a</sup> mod p  ·  Bob: B = g<sup>b</sup> mod p<br>Alice computes B<sup>a</sup> mod p, Bob computes A<sup>b</sup> mod p — both equal g<sup>ab</sup> mod p",
  "ch3.p2": "Why are they equal? Because (g<sup>b</sup>)<sup>a</sup> = g<sup>ab</sup> = (g<sup>a</sup>)<sup>b</sup>, and taking remainders preserves that identity. Eve the eavesdropper sees p, g, A, and B, but never a or b; to get g<sup>ab</sup>, the best known route is to invert A back to a — Chapter 2's hard problem.",
  "ch3.model.title": "Agreeing on a key over p = 2039",
  "ch3.model.how": "Drag Alice's and Bob's secret numbers. The middle column is everything visible on the wire. Click \"Let Eve invert\" to see how many baby-step giant-step moves she needs, or check \"Mallory in the middle\" to test a different attack.",
  "ch3.ctl.a": "Alice's secret a",
  "ch3.ctl.b": "Bob's secret b",
  "ch3.ctl.mal": "Mallory in the middle",
  "ch3.btn.eve": "Let Eve invert",
  "ch3.who.alice": "Alice",
  "ch3.who.wire": "Public wire (visible to all)",
  "ch3.who.bob": "Bob",
  "ch3.who.mal": "Mallory (interceptor)",
  "ch3.row.secret": "Private number",
  "ch3.row.sends": "Sends",
  "ch3.row.gets": "Receives",
  "ch3.row.key": "Computed key",
  "ch3.row.fromA": "Sent by Alice",
  "ch3.row.fromB": "Sent by Bob",
  "ch3.row.malsecrets": "Her two secrets",
  "ch3.row.malA": "Key with Alice",
  "ch3.row.malB": "Key with Bob",
  "ch3.read.ka": "Alice's key",
  "ch3.read.kb": "Bob's key",
  "ch3.read.eve": "Eve's recovered key",
  "ch3.read.evesteps": "Eve's steps",
  "ch3.say.ok": "Alice sends {A} and Bob sends {B}; each independently computes <em>{s}</em>. Neither {s}, nor a, nor b ever crossed the wire.",
  "ch3.say.eve": "Using baby-step giant-step, Eve needs only {steps} steps to invert A = {A} to a = {ea} and recover the same key <em>{s}</em>. Here q is only {q}, so a few dozen steps suffice; Chapter 2's race shows how fast that explodes with bit length.",
  "ch3.say.mal": "Mallory intercepts A and B and swaps in her own values. Alice ends up with <em>{ka}</em> and Bob with <em>{kb}</em>: different keys, and Mallory holds both. She decrypts Alice's messages, reads them, and re-encrypts them for Bob — neither sees anything odd, since every number on the wire looks like an ordinary integer from 1 to 2038.",
  "ch3.say.maleve": "With Mallory in the middle, Eve can still invert A and B to recover {s}, but nobody is using it: Alice uses {ka} and Bob uses {kb}, both held by Mallory.",
  "ch3.p3": "That is a <strong>man-in-the-middle attack</strong>. It solves no hard maths problem; it just stands between the two parties. Diffie–Hellman solves \"agreeing on a key in public\", not \"knowing who is on the other end\". Every real protocol therefore adds <strong>authentication</strong>: Bob must prove B really came from him. The tool is a digital signature — built with Chapter 4's RSA or Chapter 5's elliptic curves — and Chapter 6 ties signatures together with certificates.",
  "ch3.p4": "Every time you load an https page, your browser and the server perform this exact step, using an elliptic-curve version (ECDHE, Chapter 5) and much larger numbers; most modern browsers now run a post-quantum key exchange alongside it (Chapters 9–10). Our companion essay <a href=\"../../../tech/networks/how-a-web-page-arrives/\">How a Web Page Reaches Your Phone</a> shows where this step sits in the full page load.",
  "ch3.p5": "Here p = 2039 is only 11 bits long. Real finite-field Diffie–Hellman uses 2048-bit or larger p: Eve's baby-step giant-step takes around 2<sup>1024</sup> steps, and even the faster number field sieve takes around 2<sup>117</sup> operations (Chapter 7).",
  "ch3.limit": "In this toy model Mallory's two secrets are fixed. A real attacker also needs to intercept and rewrite packets in transit, which is feasible on untrusted public Wi-Fi or compromised routers — which is why authentication is mandatory.",

  /* ───── 04 ───── */
  "ch4.title": "RSA: A Public Lock",
  "ch4.law": "Two big primes are easy to multiply and hard to factor apart; RSA turns that asymmetry into a lock anyone can snap shut and only one person can open.",
  "ch4.p1": "Diffie–Hellman lets two people create a key together; RSA (proposed by Rivest, Shamir, and Adleman in 1977 and published in 1978) takes a different route: Bob publishes a \"lock\" (n, e) that anyone can use to lock a number, while only Bob with private key d can unlock it. It relies on Euler's theorem: whenever m and n are coprime, m<sup>φ(n)</sup> ≡ 1 (mod n). <a href=\"../rsa-encryption/\">RSA Public-Key Encryption · Easy to Multiply, Hard to Split</a> walks through Euler's theorem and key generation step by step; here we list the formulas and focus on three things that page left out: signatures, what factoring n gives an attacker, and why \"textbook RSA\" is unsafe on its own.",
  "ch4.eq1": "n = p·q, φ(n) = (p−1)(q−1), pick e coprime to φ(n), d = e<sup>−1</sup> mod φ(n)<br>Encrypt c = m<sup>e</sup> mod n  ·  Decrypt m = c<sup>d</sup> mod n<br>Sign s = m<sup>d</sup> mod n  ·  Verify s<sup>e</sup> mod n = m",
  "ch4.p2": "A signature simply runs encryption backwards: only Bob can compute s with d, yet anyone can check it with public e. That supplies the missing piece from Chapter 3: when Bob signs B with his private key, Mallory can no longer swap B out unnoticed.",
  "ch4.modelA.title": "A tiny RSA lock",
  "ch4.modelA.how": "Pick two distinct primes p and q and a message number m. Readouts show the public key, private key, ciphertext, decryption, and signature. Click \"Tamper ciphertext\" to see what Eve can do without decrypting, and \"Eve factors n\" to see what splitting n reveals.",
  "ch4.ctl.p": "Prime p",
  "ch4.ctl.q": "Prime q",
  "ch4.ctl.m": "Message m",
  "ch4.btn.tamper": "Tamper ciphertext (× 2ᵉ)",
  "ch4.btn.factor": "Eve factors n",
  "ch4.read.pub": "Public key (n, e)",
  "ch4.read.priv": "Private key",
  "ch4.read.c": "Ciphertext on wire",
  "ch4.read.dec": "Bob decrypts",
  "ch4.read.sig": "Bob's signature on m",
  "ch4.read.atk": "Eve factors n",
  "ch4.unit.pub": "n = {n}, e = {e}",
  "ch4.unit.sigok": "s = {s}, valid",
  "ch4.unit.sigbad": "s = {s}, failed",
  "ch4.unit.factored": "{p} × {q} ({tries} divisions)",
  "ch4.say.same": "p and q cannot match; shifted to the next prime.",
  "ch4.say.ok": "n = {p} × {q} = {n}, φ(n) = {phi}, e = {e}, d = {d}. Message m = {m} encrypts to {c}, and Bob decrypts back to <em>{dec}</em>.",
  "ch4.say.tamper": "Eve cannot read {c}, yet she multiplies it by 2<sup>e</sup> mod n and sends {ct}. Bob decrypts normally and gets <em>{dec}</em> — exactly double. If m is a bank transfer amount, Eve doubled it without ever learning m.",
  "ch4.say.tamperwrap": "Eve multiplies the ciphertext by 2<sup>e</sup>; Bob decrypts <em>{dec}</em>, which is 2 × {m} minus n. Eve still controls the change, only wrapped modulo n.",
  "ch4.say.factor": "Eve trial-divides n = {n} from 3 upwards and splits it in {tries} divisions. Knowing p and q, she computes φ(n) and d = {d} just like Bob — the private key is completely exposed. This n has only {digits} decimal digits.",
  "ch4.say.both": "Eve has already factored n ({tries} divisions) and recovered d = {d}, so tampering is pointless: she can read m = {m} directly and forge Bob's signatures.",
  "ch4.p3": "Why does tampering work? Because (2m)<sup>e</sup> = 2<sup>e</sup>·m<sup>e</sup>: textbook RSA is multiplicatively homomorphic, so scaling the ciphertext scales the plaintext. Worse, the same m always encrypts to the same c, letting Eve encrypt candidate guesses and compare against c on the wire.",
  "ch4.p4": "Real RSA therefore never encrypts m raw; it first \"pads\" m with random bytes in a rigid format (OAEP, RFC 8017), and signatures hash and pad too (PSS). The next demo shows an even simpler break when e is tiny and the message is short: no factoring needed at all.",
  "ch4.modelB.title": "When e = 3, a cube root is enough",
  "ch4.modelB.how": "Here n is 512 bits, generated live in the page. Pick a short message to see whether its cube reaches n, then check \"Add random padding\".",
  "ch4.ctl.msg": "Message",
  "ch4.ctl.pad": "Add random padding",
  "ch4.read.n": "Modulus n",
  "ch4.read.m": "Encoded integer",
  "ch4.read.cc": "Ciphertext c = m³ mod n",
  "ch4.read.out": "Eve's cube root reads",
  "ch4.unit.noroot": "Not a perfect cube — attack fails",
  "ch4.say.cube": "“{msg}” as an integer is only {mbits} bits long, and its cube is {cubebits} bits — far smaller than the {bits}-bit n. So \"mod n\" never triggers: the ciphertext is literally m³, and taking an integer cube root hands Eve <em>“{msg}”</em>.",
  "ch4.say.pad": "With padding, the integer is {pbits} bits long, so its cube far exceeds n and modular reduction scrambles it. Eve's cube root is no longer an integer and fails; Bob decrypts with d, strips the padding, and still reads “{msg}”.",
  "ch4.p5": "Both demos make the same point: <strong>the hard problem is only the foundation</strong>; how you wrap it (padding, randomisation, signature formatting) decides whether the system holds. Real RSA uses at least a 2048-bit n — 617 decimal digits — whereas the toy default above has just 5.",
  "ch4.limit": "The toy RSA above uses zero padding and primes small enough for instant trial division; it exists solely to show the algebra. Real implementations also have to guard against timing and other side-channel leaks, a discipline of its own.",

  /* ───── 05 ───── */
  "ch5.title": "Elliptic Curves: Shorter Keys, Same Difficulty",
  "ch5.law": "Point addition on an elliptic curve is just as fast forward and hard backwards, yet has no known number-field-sieve shortcut — so the same security takes far shorter keys.",
  "ch5.p1": "Chapters 2 to 4 all lived inside multiplication modulo p. Around 1985, Neal Koblitz and Victor Miller independently proposed moving to a different setting: points on an elliptic curve, given by",
  "ch5.eq1": "y² = x³ + a·x + b",
  "ch5.modelA.title": "Point addition over real numbers: y² = x³ − 2x + 2",
  "ch5.modelA.how": "Drag the x-coordinates of P and Q. The line through P and Q almost always meets the curve at a third point (grey); flip it vertically across the x-axis to get P + Q (gold). Set both x-coordinates equal to see the tangent, or check \"Q on lower branch\" to make the line vertical.",
  "ch5.modelA.aria": "Real elliptic curve with line through P and Q and sum P + Q",
  "ch5.leg.third": "Third intersection with curve",
  "ch5.ctl.xp": "x-coordinate of P",
  "ch5.ctl.xq": "x-coordinate of Q",
  "ch5.ctl.qneg": "Q on lower branch",
  "ch5.read.slope": "Line slope",
  "ch5.unit.inf": "Point at infinity O",
  "ch5.say.add": "The line through P and Q has slope {s}; flipping its third intersection across the x-axis gives P + Q = <em>{R}</em>.",
  "ch5.say.double": "When P and Q coincide, the secant becomes the tangent with slope {s}, giving 2P = <em>{R}</em> — \"point doubling\".",
  "ch5.say.inf": "P and Q are mirror images, so the line through them is vertical and never hits the curve again. By definition it meets at the \"point at infinity O\", so P + Q = O: O acts as zero in this addition.",
  "ch5.say.off": "(This point lies outside the plotted window.)",
  "ch5.p2": "This rule forms a <strong>group</strong>: addition is associative, has an identity (O), and every point has an inverse (vertical reflection). We can therefore repeat addition like exponentiation: kP = P + P + … + P. Forward computation uses \"double-and-add\", the exact twin of Chapter 2's square-and-multiply with point addition replacing multiplication.",
  "ch5.p3": "Real numbers have infinitely many points and rounding errors, so they cannot be used for encryption. Move the exact same formulas to remainders modulo a prime p (replacing division with modular inverses), and the smooth curve turns into a scatter of grid points.",
  "ch5.modelB.title": "Curve modulo p: y² = x³ + 2x + 3",
  "ch5.modelB.how": "Drag prime p to see how many points the curve holds, and drag k to watch G, 2G, 3G… hop around the grid. Gold marks kG.",
  "ch5.modelB.aria": "Elliptic curve points modulo p with jumping trajectory G, 2G, 3G…",
  "ch5.leg.pt": "Curve points",
  "ch5.leg.jump": "Hops G, 2G, 3G…",
  "ch5.ctl.p": "Prime p",
  "ch5.ctl.k": "k (in kG)",
  "ch5.read.n": "Points on curve (incl. O)",
  "ch5.read.hasse": "Hasse theorem bound",
  "ch5.read.kg": "kG",
  "ch5.read.ops": "Double-and-add / naive steps",
  "ch5.unit.ops": "{a} / {b}",
  "ch5.say.cyclic": "At p = {p}, the curve has {N} points (including O), and G = ({Gx}, {Gy}) generates all {ord} of them. For k = {k}, double-and-add reaches kG = <em>({x}, {y})</em> in just {ops} point operations; seeing only G and kG, Eve adding one step at a time would need {k} additions.",
  "ch5.say.sub": "At p = {p}, the curve has {N} points (including O), inside Hasse's interval {lo}–{hi}; G = ({Gx}, {Gy}) visits {ord} of them (the order of G). For k = {k}, double-and-add reaches kG = <em>({x}, {y})</em> in {ops} operations, versus {k} steps one by one.",
  "ch5.say.ord": "k = {k} equals the order of G: kG lands back on the point at infinity O, and adding further cycles through G again.",
  "ch5.p4": "Finding k from G and kG is the <strong>elliptic-curve discrete logarithm</strong>. On well-chosen curves, the best known general algorithm is Pollard's ρ method: like baby-step giant-step in Chapter 2 it needs roughly √n steps (where n is the order of G), using almost no memory. The crucial difference is that modular multiplication has the sub-exponential number field sieve, whereas elliptic curves have no known counterpart. So an elliptic-curve key only needs twice as many bits as the desired security level, while RSA must be an order of magnitude longer. Compare NIST's table:",
  "ch5.th.s": "Security strength (bits)",
  "ch5.th.rsa": "RSA / finite-field DH (bits)",
  "ch5.th.ecc": "Elliptic curve (bits)",
  "ch5.tcap": "Source: NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 Rev. 5 (2020), Table 2. \"Security strength\" is roughly the base-2 log of operations to break. Chapter 7 recomputes this table from formulas.",
  "ch5.p5": "Thus a 256-bit curve (such as P-256 or Curve25519) matches 3072-bit RSA at the 128-bit security tier. Keys are compact, computation is fast, and less data crosses the wire; TLS 1.3 normally uses elliptic-curve Diffie–Hellman (ECDHE), most commonly on X25519.",
  "ch5.limit": "Primes here stay at or below 251 so every point is visible; real curves use ~256-bit primes (Curve25519 uses 2<sup>255</sup> − 19) with roughly 2<sup>255</sup> points. Curves must also be chosen carefully: special curves with shortcuts (such as pairing-friendly curves that map back into finite fields) are avoided in standard key exchange.",

  /* ───── 06 ───── */
  "ch6.title": "Signatures and Certificates: Who Are You Talking To?",
  "ch6.law": "A signature answers \"did that key say this?\"; a certificate delegates \"whose key is this?\" to an authority you already trust.",
  "ch6.p1": "Back to Chapter 3's leftover question: did B really come from Bob? Bob signs B (and the handshake transcript) with his private key. Because a signature must cover data of any length while RSA only handles numbers below n, we first compress the message with a <strong>hash function</strong> into a fixed-length \"fingerprint\" and sign the fingerprint (<a href=\"../../algorithms/hashing/\">Hashing · Squeezing Any Input Into a Fixed Fingerprint</a> covers fingerprints and collisions).",
  "ch6.eq1": "Signature s = H(m)<sup>d</sup> mod n  ·  Verify: does s<sup>e</sup> mod n equal H(m)?",
  "ch6.p2": "One new question pops up at once: how do you know that public key belongs to Bob and not Mallory? That is what a <strong>certificate</strong> does: a statement saying \"the name is shop.example, the public key is…\", signed by an authority you already trust. That authority's key is signed by a higher authority, up to a \"root certificate\" pre-installed in your operating system or browser. The padlock in the address bar means the entire chain verified from top to bottom.",
  "ch6.model.title": "A three-link certificate chain",
  "ch6.model.how": "This widget generates fresh 512-bit RSA keys and builds a SHA-256 signed chain. Pick a tampering mode and watch which link fails verification.",
  "ch6.ctl.tamper": "Tampering mode",
  "ch6.t.none": "Intact",
  "ch6.t.name": "Rename website",
  "ch6.t.key": "Swap in Mallory's key",
  "ch6.t.resign": "Mallory swaps key & re-signs",
  "ch6.t.selfroot": "Mallory's self-made root",
  "ch6.c.root": "Root certificate",
  "ch6.c.inter": "Intermediate certificate",
  "ch6.c.site": "Website certificate",
  "ch6.c.subject": "Subject:",
  "ch6.c.issuer": "Issuer:",
  "ch6.c.key": "Key prefix:",
  "ch6.c.hash": "SHA-256 prefix:",
  "ch6.c.sigok": "✓ Signature verified with \"{by}\" key",
  "ch6.c.sigbad": "✗ Signature failed against \"{by}\" key",
  "ch6.c.trusted": "✓ In browser's pre-installed trust store",
  "ch6.c.untrusted": "✗ Key does not match pre-installed trust store",
  "ch6.verdict.ok": "Full chain verified: browser displays the padlock.",
  "ch6.verdict.bad": "Chain broken: browser refuses the connection and shows a warning.",
  "ch6.say.none": "Every certificate's fingerprint verifies under the issuer key above it, and the root key matches the browser's pre-installed trust store.",
  "ch6.say.name": "Changing shop.example to bank.example alters the SHA-256 fingerprint completely, so the intermediate CA's signature no longer matches. Changing one letter breaks it just as thoroughly as changing a paragraph.",
  "ch6.say.key": "Swapping in Mallory's public key changes the certificate payload, breaking the existing signature. This is the exact substitution Mallory wanted in Chapter 3, and the signature stops it.",
  "ch6.say.resign": "Mallory swaps in her key and signs the certificate with her own private key. The signature itself is valid under her key, but verification checks it against the intermediate CA's public key and rejects it. Without the CA's private key she cannot forge a valid link.",
  "ch6.say.selfroot": "Mallory builds her own \"root certificate\" and signs the whole chain beneath it: every link verifies internally, yet her root key is absent from the browser's pre-installed store, failing at step one. All trust ultimately anchors in that pre-installed list.",
  "ch6.p3": "TLS 1.3 (the protocol behind https, RFC 8446) stitches the previous chapters into four steps:",
  "ch6.tls1": "The browser sends a public elliptic-curve share (Chapter 5's ECDHE); most modern browsers now include a post-quantum public key alongside it (Chapter 9).",
  "ch6.tls2": "The server returns its own public share, and both derive the same shared secret (Chapter 3). Everything that follows is encrypted with symmetric keys derived from it.",
  "ch6.tls3": "The server sends its certificate chain and signs <strong>the entire handshake transcript so far</strong> with the certificate's private key (CertificateVerify). The browser checks both the chain above and that signature, binding the agreed key to the server's name and blocking a man in the middle.",
  "ch6.tls4": "Both sides exchange authentication tags computed from the new keys (Finished) to confirm nothing was altered, then start sending the web page.",
  "ch6.p4": "Notice the division of labour: key exchange uses ephemeral numbers generated for one session and thrown away, so even if the server's long-term private key leaks years later, recorded past traffic cannot be decrypted (forward secrecy); the certificate's long-term key is used solely for signing. Chapter 8 explains why that split matters against a quantum threat.",
  "ch6.limit": "The keys here are only 512 bits (factored publicly back in 1999), signatures have no padding, and real certificates also carry expiry dates, revocation checks, and Certificate Transparency logs.",

  /* ───── 07 ───── */
  "ch7.title": "How Safe Is \"Safe\"?",
  "ch7.law": "\"Security\" is a number: the base-2 logarithm of the operations required by the best known attack, measured in bits of security.",
  "ch7.p1": "AES-128 has 2<sup>128</sup> keys and no known attack markedly better than trying them, so it provides 128-bit security. For public-key schemes, key length and bits of security are not the same number — it depends on the best known attack. For RSA and finite-field discrete logs, that is the <strong>general number field sieve</strong>, whose cost scales roughly as",
  "ch7.eq1": "L(n) = exp( (64/9)<sup>1/3</sup> · (ln n)<sup>1/3</sup> · (ln ln n)<sup>2/3</sup> )",
  "ch7.p2": "The exponent grows as the cube root of ln n, much slower than brute force (\"sub-exponential\"), which is why RSA keys must be an order of magnitude longer than their security level. Elliptic curves face only Pollard's ρ at roughly √(πn/4), so curve length is about twice the security level.",
  "ch7.model.title": "Key length vs attack cost",
  "ch7.model.how": "Horizontal axis is key length (log scale); vertical axis is log₂ of attack operations, with a dashed reference at 128 bits. Drag key length to read all three curves; white dots show the NIST table and red dots show public factoring and discrete-log records. Check \"Quantum computer\" for a preview of Chapter 8.",
  "ch7.model.aria": "Attack cost versus key length for symmetric ciphers, elliptic curves, and RSA",
  "ch7.leg.sym": "Symmetric cipher (brute force)",
  "ch7.leg.ecc": "Elliptic curve (Pollard ρ)",
  "ch7.leg.rsa": "RSA, finite-field DH (number field sieve)",
  "ch7.leg.nist": "NIST SP 800-57 table",
  "ch7.leg.rec": "Public records",
  "ch7.leg.q": "Quantum attack (growth shape only)",
  "ch7.ctl.bits": "Key length",
  "ch7.ctl.q": "Quantum computer",
  "ch7.read.sym": "Symmetric: brute force",
  "ch7.read.ecc": "Elliptic curve: ρ method",
  "ch7.read.rsa": "RSA: number field sieve",
  "ch7.read.years": "Time to factor this RSA at 10¹⁸ ops/s",
  "ch7.cv.x": "Key length (bits, log scale) →",
  "ch7.cv.y": "Attack cost (log₂ operations)",
  "ch7.unit.years": "about {v} yr",
  "ch7.say.sym": "{k}-bit symmetric key: brute force takes 2<sup>{sym}</sup> operations. That length is far too short to be meaningful for elliptic curves or RSA.",
  "ch7.say.symecc": "{k} bits: symmetric brute force needs 2<sup>{sym}</sup> operations; an elliptic curve of the same length yields ~2<sup>{ecc}</sup> under Pollard ρ — half as many bits. Hence a 256-bit curve gives 128-bit security.",
  "ch7.say.ecc": "{k}-bit elliptic curve: Pollard ρ takes ~2<sup>{ecc}</sup> operations.",
  "ch7.say.both": "{k} bits: as an elliptic curve, ~2<sup>{ecc}</sup> operations; as RSA, the number field sieve formula gives only ~2<sup>{rsa}</sup> operations — far too weak.",
  "ch7.say.rsa": "{k}-bit RSA: the number field sieve formula gives ~2<sup>{rsa}</sup> operations.",
  "ch7.say.qpk": "<em>Quantum computer:</em> Shor's algorithm collapses RSA and elliptic curves to polynomial time. The dashed line plots only the n³ scaling shape (~2<sup>{shor}</sup> without constants) — making keys a few times longer cannot save them.",
  "ch7.say.qsym": "<em>Quantum computer:</em> Grover's algorithm takes the square root of brute force, ~2<sup>{gro}</sup> sequential iterations. Doubling symmetric key length restores the margin.",
  "ch7.p3": "Compare the formula against real records. Dropping the \"o(1)\" correction term leaves an order-of-magnitude guide: at the five NIST points, the formula runs 5 to 13 bits higher than the table. Here are the public records, all achieved with the number field sieve:",
  "ch7.th.what": "Record",
  "ch7.th.bits": "Bits",
  "ch7.th.year": "Year",
  "ch7.th.formula": "Formula estimate",
  "ch7.rec.rsa768": "RSA-768 factoring (Kleinjung et al., Dec 2009)",
  "ch7.rec.dlp795": "795-bit prime-field discrete log (Boudot et al., Dec 2019)",
  "ch7.rec.rsa250": "RSA-250 factoring (Boudot et al., Feb 2020)",
  "ch7.rec.rsa260": "RSA-260 factoring (GPU CADO-NFS, 3 Sep 2026)",
  "ch7.rec.rsa896": "RSA-896 factoring (GPU CADO-NFS, 19 Sep 2026)",
  "ch7.tcap": "Sources for the records appear in Chapter 10 and the footer. The last column is the order-of-magnitude estimate from the formula above, not the actual operation count those runs spent.",
  "ch7.p4": "Two takeaways follow. First, the records remain far below today's 2048-bit standard: the formula gives ~2<sup>117</sup> operations for 2048 bits, roughly 2<sup>35</sup>× higher than ~2<sup>82</sup> for 896 bits. Second, records keep advancing, and the two September 2026 milestones came mainly from GPUs and sharper engineering rather than a new algorithm (as RSA-896's author explicitly noted). He also argued that an actor with data-centre-scale GPUs can already threaten 1024-bit RSA — his judgement; the formula gives ~2<sup>87</sup> for 1024 bits and NIST rates it at 80 bits.",
  "ch7.limit": "The formula gives only order of magnitude: constants, memory, and parallelism are omitted, and real records are billed in core-years or GPU-years rather than abstract operations. \"10<sup>18</sup> ops/s\" is a round-number illustration, not any real machine.",

  /* ───── 08 ───── */
  "ch8.title": "What Would a Quantum Computer Change?",
  "ch8.law": "A quantum computer does not break all cryptography: it turns factoring and discrete logs from hard to easy, while only discounting symmetric keys.",
  "ch8.p1": "In 1994, Peter Shor proved that a large enough quantum computer can factor integers and compute discrete logarithms in polynomial time — breaking the two problems behind RSA, Diffie–Hellman, and elliptic curves. The quantum computer performs just one step: finding a <strong>period</strong>. Our essay <a href=\"../../quantum/what-quantum-computers-do/\">What Quantum Computers Can Actually Do · From First Principles to the Frontier</a> shows how quantum interference finds periods; here we look at the classical number theory wrapped around it, turning \"find a period\" into \"factor N\":",
  "ch8.eq1": "Pick any a and find the smallest r with a<sup>r</sup> ≡ 1 (mod N). If r is even and y = a<sup>r/2</sup> mod N ≠ N − 1, then gcd(y − 1, N) and gcd(y + 1, N) are non-trivial factors of N.",
  "ch8.modelA.title": "The classical shell around Shor's algorithm: factoring N from a period",
  "ch8.modelA.how": "Pick N and a. The plot traces a<sup>x</sup> mod N (x = 0, 1, 2…), repeating every r steps, with gold dots marking each cycle start. Here the browser finds r by slow trial search — the exact step a quantum computer replaces.",
  "ch8.modelA.aria": "Periodic sequence of a^x mod N repeating every r steps",
  "ch8.ctl.n": "N",
  "ch8.ctl.a": "a",
  "ch8.read.r": "Period r",
  "ch8.read.y": "y = a^(r/2) mod N",
  "ch8.read.f": "Factors found",
  "ch8.read.share": "Share of a that succeed on first try",
  "ch8.cv.x": "x →",
  "ch8.cv.y": "aˣ mod N",
  "ch8.say.ok": "a = {a} has even period r = {r}; y = {a}<sup>{h}</sup> mod {n} = {y}. Then gcd({y} − 1, {n}) = {f1} and gcd({y} + 1, {n}) = {f2}, yielding <em>{n} = {f1} × {f2}</em>.",
  "ch8.say.odd": "a = {a} has odd period r = {r}, which cannot be halved. Pick another a: for {n}, {ok} out of {tot} coprime choices ({share}%) succeed on the spot.",
  "ch8.say.minus1": "a = {a} has period r = {r}, yet a<sup>r/2</sup> mod {n} = {y} equals N − 1, so both gcds give only 1 and N. Try another a.",
  "ch8.say.lucky": "a = {a} already shares a factor with {n}, so gcd immediately gives {f1} × {f2} without needing the period. For huge N such luck essentially never happens.",
  "ch8.p2": "For a product of two distinct odd primes, a random coprime a succeeds with probability at least 1/2 (a standard result in Nielsen & Chuang, Chapter 5), so a handful of tries suffices. Classical machines stall on finding r, which can be nearly as large as N — searching one by one is Chapter 2's brute force. A quantum computer uses the quantum Fourier transform to read out r in polynomial steps, and the same idea solves discrete logs, elliptic curves included.",
  "ch8.p3": "Symmetric ciphers face only Grover's algorithm, which takes a square root — turning 2<sup>128</sup> into roughly 2<sup>64</sup> iterations. That sounds scary until you note those iterations are strictly sequential and cannot be split across a million machines like classical search; NIST's post-quantum evaluation estimates breaking AES-128 takes ~2<sup>170</sup>/MAXDEPTH quantum gates (where MAXDEPTH caps circuit depth). The standard conclusion is that symmetric ciphers and hashes need only stay at or move up to 256 bits; what must actually be replaced is public-key cryptography. Glance back at Chapter 7's chart with \"Quantum computer\" checked to see the divide.",
  "ch8.p4": "When does the danger arrive? Separate three things clearly. <span class=\"tag h\">Established maths</span>Shor's algorithm works on an ideal quantum computer — that is a theorem. <span class=\"tag c\">Demonstrated</span>As of November 2026, published quantum factoring demos have only factored numbers tiny enough to split by hand (Chapter 10). <span class=\"tag m\">Estimates & forecasts</span>How large a machine is needed and when it might be built are estimates — Chapter 10 lists several with their authors. One risk does not wait, however: encrypted traffic recorded today can be stored and decrypted later — <strong>harvest now, decrypt later</strong>.",
  "ch8.modelB.title": "Mosca's inequality: should migration start now?",
  "ch8.modelB.how": "x: years data must stay secret; y: years to migrate to post-quantum cryptography; z: years until a cryptographically relevant quantum computer (red line). Whenever x + y > z, data encrypted before migration finishes will be exposed while it still needs secrecy.",
  "ch8.ctl.x": "Secrecy shelf life x",
  "ch8.ctl.y": "Migration time y",
  "ch8.ctl.z": "Assumed arrival z",
  "ch8.unit.yr": "{v} yr",
  "ch8.bar.mig": "Migration",
  "ch8.bar.data": "Data secrecy",
  "ch8.bar.q": "Quantum computer",
  "ch8.read.exp": "Years exposed x + y − z",
  "ch8.read.start": "Margin to start",
  "ch8.unit.slack": "{v} yr left",
  "ch8.unit.late": "{v} yr late",
  "ch8.say.safe": "x + y = {x} + {y} sits {sl} yr below z = {z}: under these assumptions, data encrypted before migration finishes expires before a quantum computer arrives.",
  "ch8.say.edge": "x + y equals z exactly: zero margin — any slip in any estimate exposes data.",
  "ch8.say.exposed": "x + y = {x} + {y} overshoots z = {z}: data encrypted before migration finishes will still need secrecy for {ex} yr after the quantum computer arrives. Recorded today, it can be decrypted then.",
  "ch8.p5": "The default z is not my forecast — it is just a slider starting point you can drag anywhere. Mosca's inequality says that even if you expect quantum computers to take many years, long-lived secrets plus slow migrations mean starting now. Most national migration deadlines listed in Chapter 10 fall between 2030 and 2035.",
  "ch8.limit": "Mosca's inequality is only a comparison; you supply all three numbers. Signatures behave differently: forging a signature can only happen after a quantum computer exists, so \"harvest now, decrypt later\" does not apply to them. That is why US federal deadlines swap key exchange first (end of 2030) and signatures second (end of 2031), as detailed in Chapter 10.",

  /* ───── 09 ───── */
  "ch9.title": "Lattices: Hiding a Secret Inside Noise",
  "ch9.law": "The main post-quantum replacements hide secrets in lattices: a good basis makes a problem easy, a bad basis makes it hard, and a dash of noise defeats linear algebra.",
  "ch9.p1": "Quantum computers excel at period finding, so we need a hard problem with no hidden period. Both of NIST's flagship 2024 standards — ML-KEM for key encapsulation and ML-DSA for signatures — rest on <strong>lattices</strong>. A lattice is an infinite regular grid of points: take two vectors b₁ and b₂, and form all integer combinations k₁b₁ + k₂b₂. Many different pairs of basis vectors generate the exact same grid.",
  "ch9.modelA.title": "One lattice, a good basis and a bad basis",
  "ch9.modelA.how": "Grey dots are lattice points. Green arrows form a nearly orthogonal \"good basis\"; red arrows form a skewed \"bad basis\" for the exact same lattice (drag the skew slider to flatten it). The purple dot is the target, the white ring is the truly nearest lattice point, green and red dots are each basis's rounded guess, and the shaded parallelograms show each basis's rounding cell.",
  "ch9.modelA.aria": "2D lattice with good and bad bases and their closest-vector rounding results",
  "ch9.leg.t": "Target",
  "ch9.leg.good": "Good-basis rounding",
  "ch9.leg.bad": "Bad-basis rounding",
  "ch9.leg.gb": "Good basis",
  "ch9.leg.bb": "Bad basis",
  "ch9.ctl.k": "Bad-basis skew",
  "ch9.btn.new": "New target",
  "ch9.read.good": "Good-basis distance to target",
  "ch9.read.bad": "Bad-basis distance to target",
  "ch9.read.had": "Bad-basis Hadamard ratio (1 = orthogonal)",
  "ch9.read.rate": "Bad-basis hit rate over 2,000 targets",
  "ch9.say.split": "The nearest lattice point sits at distance {d}. Good-basis rounding hits it; the bad basis lands {db} away — {x}× farther. Both bases span the exact same lattice (identical determinant); the only difference is usability.",
  "ch9.say.both": "This time both bases found the nearest point (distance {d}). Try more targets: across 2,000 random targets, the good basis succeeds {rg}% of the time, while this bad basis hits only {rb}%.",
  "ch9.say.neither": "A tricky target: good-basis distance is {dg} and bad-basis distance is {db}, while the true nearest point is {d}. Even the good basis is slightly off orthogonal (Hadamard ratio {hg}), so it can miss too; over 2,000 random targets it gets {rg}% vs {rb}% for the bad basis.",
  "ch9.say.badonly": "Here the bad basis happened to hit while the good basis missed ({db} vs {dg}). That is rare: over 2,000 random targets, the good basis succeeds {rg}% vs {rb}%.",
  "ch9.p2": "This is the <strong>closest vector problem</strong> (CVP). \"Rounding\" is Babai's 1986 method: express the target in basis coordinates and round each coefficient. The closer the basis is to orthogonal, the smaller the rounding error. In 2D anyone can turn a bad basis into a good one (lattice basis reduction, such as LLL), so 2D is trivial. What is hard is hundreds of dimensions: the best known reduction algorithms fail to recover a short orthogonal basis in high dimensions in less than exponential time, and no known quantum algorithm does markedly better. Using a good basis as the private key and a bad basis as the public key gives one family of lattice schemes; ML-KEM uses an equivalent formulation called <strong>learning with errors</strong> (LWE, Regev 2005):",
  "ch9.eq1": "Given a random matrix A and b = A·s + e (mod q), where e is small random noise, find s",
  "ch9.p3": "Without e, that is high-school linear algebra; add a sliver of noise and it becomes a hard lattice problem. Below is Regev's original encryption scheme running live in your browser: Bob's public key is (A, b). To encrypt one bit, Alice sums a random subset of rows and hides the bit in an offset of q/2; Bob uses s to cancel A, leaving only the summed noise plus that offset.",
  "ch9.modelB.title": "A working LWE cipher (Regev's scheme)",
  "ch9.modelB.how": "The page generates secret s and public key (A, b), then encrypts and decrypts 2,000 random bits. Decryption error equals the summed noise, which must stay inside the two red dashed lines (±q/4) to decode right. Drag dimension and noise η, switch modulus q, click \"Let Eve solve equations\", and check \"Remove noise\" to compare.",
  "ch9.modelB.aria": "Histogram of accumulated LWE decryption noise across 2,000 trials against ±q/4 threshold",
  "ch9.leg.hist": "Accumulated noise over 2,000 decryptions",
  "ch9.leg.thr": "±q/4: outside this range fails",
  "ch9.ctl.n": "Dimension n",
  "ch9.ctl.eta": "Noise size η",
  "ch9.ctl.q": "Modulus q",
  "ch9.btn.eve": "Let Eve solve equations",
  "ch9.ctl.zero": "Remove noise",
  "ch9.read.fail": "Failures in 2,000 trials",
  "ch9.read.pred": "Predicted failure rate for this key's noise",
  "ch9.read.brute": "Choices for Eve to brute-force s",
  "ch9.read.lin": "Eve's Gaussian elimination on s",
  "ch9.unit.nm": "n = {n}, samples m = {m}",
  "ch9.unit.tiny": "< 0.01%",
  "ch9.unit.linok": "Exact match: secret exposed",
  "ch9.unit.linbad": "{w} of {n} coordinates wrong",
  "ch9.cv.x": "Accumulated noise (centred mod q) →",
  "ch9.cv.y": "Count",
  "ch9.say.zero": "With noise removed, b = A·s is an ordinary linear system. Decryption never fails, yet Eve picks n equations, runs Gaussian elimination, and recovers s immediately.",
  "ch9.say.clean": "Accumulated noise has width ~{sd} (centred around {mu}), safely inside ±{q4}, so all {tr} decryptions <em>succeeded with zero errors</em>. At q = {q} and n = {n}, brute-forcing s spans 10<sup>{brute}</sup> possibilities.",
  "ch9.say.some": "<em>{fail} failures</em> out of {tr} decryptions ({pct}%), matching the ~{pred}% rate predicted from this key's noise. With width ~{sd} and shift {mu}, rare tails cross ±{q4}. Real schemes drive this failure rate to negligible levels (~2<sup>−164</sup> in ML-KEM-768).",
  "ch9.say.many": "<em>{fail} failures</em> out of {tr} decryptions ({pct}%): width ~{sd} and shift {mu} spill widely past ±{q4}. Too much noise breaks decryption for Bob himself — lower η or pick a larger q.",
  "ch9.say.eveok": "Eve's Gaussian elimination recovers s completely.",
  "ch9.say.evebad": "Eve runs Gaussian elimination anyway, but every equation carries small noise that elimination amplifies: {w} coordinates of her solved s are wrong, rendering it useless.",
  "ch9.p4": "This model lays bare lattice cryptography's core trade-off: larger noise makes life harder for Eve yet raises Bob's chance of a decryption error; higher dimension helps security yet sums more noise (here m = 4n samples). One more subtlety: for a fixed key, Regev's summed noise is centred not at 0 but at half the key's own noise sum, so different keys at the same settings have different failure rates; ML-KEM adds small signed noise during encryption as well to avoid that drift. Brute force at n = 8, q = 97 already spans ~10<sup>16</sup> keys, though real attacks use lattice basis reduction — finding a good basis from a bad one as in the previous model.",
  "ch9.p5": "ML-KEM (FIPS 203) turns this into a practical standard: entries of s, e, and A become polynomials in Z<sub>q</sub>[X]/(X<sup>256</sup>+1) with q = 3329, shrinking matrices to k×k blocks — <strong>module-LWE</strong> — to save vast space and time. Noise follows a centred binomial distribution (like η above), ciphertexts are compressed, and the Fujisaki–Okamoto transform wraps public-key encryption into a key-encapsulation mechanism (KEM) safe against active attackers. Three parameter sets:",
  "ch9.th.name": "Name",
  "ch9.th.dim": "Dimension (k × 256)",
  "ch9.th.ek": "Public key (bytes)",
  "ch9.th.ct": "Ciphertext (bytes)",
  "ch9.th.cat": "NIST level",
  "ch9.tcap": "Byte sizes follow FIPS 203 formulas (384k + 32 for public keys and 32(k·d<sub>u</sub> + d<sub>v</sub>) for ciphertexts), matching Go's standard library constants. Levels 1, 3, and 5 target at least the difficulty of breaking AES-128, AES-192, and AES-256. All three output a 32-byte shared key.",
  "ch9.p6": "Compared with elliptic curves, the price is size: an X25519 public key is 32 bytes, whereas ML-KEM-768 takes 1,184 bytes plus a 1,088-byte ciphertext. That takes us straight to the performance table in Chapter 10.",
  "ch9.limit": "Dimensions here top out at 32 versus 768 for ML-KEM-768; noise, modulus, and sample counts are tuned so failures are visible rather than matching any standard. Raw Regev encrypts one bit at a time and lacks the FO transform against active attacks.",

  /* ───── 10 ───── */
  "ch10.title": "Where the Frontier Stands (as of November 2026)",
  "ch10.law": "The standards are published and large-scale deployment is under way; what remains open is when a capable quantum computer arrives and whether any new scheme collapses suddenly like SIKE.",
  "ch10.p1": "Every item below carries its date and source (full URLs and access dates live in the footer's sources list and this directory's BRIEF.md). They fall into four buckets: standards, deployment, attacks and records, and <strong>estimates and forecasts</strong>. That last bucket consists of someone's claim rather than established fact, and names who said it.",
  "ch10.g.std": "Standards: published and in progress",
  "ch10.g.deploy": "Deployment: already in use",
  "ch10.g.attack": "Attacks & records: already happened",
  "ch10.g.claim": "Estimates & forecasts: someone's claim",
  "fx.fips": "NIST published three post-quantum standards: FIPS 203 (ML-KEM, lattice key encapsulation, from Kyber), FIPS 204 (ML-DSA, lattice signatures, from Dilithium), and FIPS 205 (SLH-DSA, stateless hash-based signatures, from SPHINCS+). (NIST press release, 13 Aug 2024)",
  "fx.hqc": "NIST selected HQC (error-correcting codes) as a backup to ML-KEM for mathematical diversity, stating explicitly that it does not replace ML-KEM. NIST IR 8547 (Oct 2026) notes that a FIPS specifying HQC is in development. (NIST release, 11 Mar 2025; NIST IR 8547)",
  "fx.fndsa": "FN-DSA (from Falcon, offering shorter signatures) is assigned FIPS 206, presented at NIST's 6th PQC Standardisation Conference in September 2025; as of November 2026 no public draft was posted on NIST's site. (NIST presentation, 25 Sep 2025)",
  "fx.sigs": "To avoid putting all signature eggs in the lattice basket, NIST opened an additional signature competition; on 14 May 2026 it advanced 9 candidates to Round 3: FAEST, HAWK, MAYO, MQOM, QR-UOV, SDitH, SNOVA, SQIsign, and UOV. (NIST notice & NIST IR 8610)",
  "fx.ir8547": "NIST IR 8547 finalised: quantum-vulnerable public-key algorithms (RSA, elliptic curves, finite-field DH) become deprecated after 2030 and disallowed under NIST standards after 2035, with ML-KEM-768 as the default recommendation. Under US Executive Order 14412 and OMB M-26-15, high-value federal systems must migrate key establishment by end of 2030 and signatures by end of 2031. (NIST, 27 Oct 2026)",
  "fx.rfc": "IETF published RFC 10024 (Standards Track), specifying hybrid \"classical + post-quantum\" key exchange such as X25519MLKEM768 for TLS 1.3. (IETF, Aug 2026)",
  "fx.tls": "Cloudflare reported that ~70% of browser traffic reaching its network already uses hybrid ML-KEM key agreement, compared with ~15% on connections to origin servers. (Cloudflare blog, 29 Sep 2026)",
  "fx.browsers": "Browsers enabling hybrid ML-KEM by default: Chrome and Edge from v131, Firefox desktop from v132, Safari from v26, plus OS-level support in Apple iOS 26 and macOS Tahoe 26. Post-quantum <strong>signatures</strong> are not yet enabled by default in major browsers. (Cloudflare dev docs, updated Jun 2026; Google Security Blog, Sep 2024; Apple support)",
  "fx.msg": "Messaging: Signal deployed PQXDH in September 2023 and added continuous key ratcheting via SPQR in October 2025 (forming its \"Triple Ratchet\"); Apple introduced iMessage PQ3 in February 2024. (Signal blog; Apple Security Blog)",
  "fx.os": "OS & tooling: OpenSSH 10.0 (9 Apr 2025) enabled hybrid mlkem768x25519 by default; OpenSSL 3.5 added default hybrid ML-KEM and PQ signature support; Microsoft announced GA of ML-KEM and ML-DSA in Windows 11, Windows Server 2025, and .NET 10 on 18 Nov 2025. (OpenSSH release notes; Cloudflare dev docs; Microsoft Security Blog)",
  "fx.gov": "Migration timelines: UK NCSC (Mar 2025) sets discovery by 2028, priority migrations by 2031, and full completion by 2035; the EU coordinated roadmap (Jun 2025) sets initial foundations by end of 2026, high-risk use cases by end of 2030, and remaining transitions by end of 2035; US federal deadlines appear under NIST IR 8547 above. (NCSC; European Commission; PQShield summary)",
  "fx.sike": "SIKE (based on supersingular isogenies, a NIST Round 4 finalist) was broken classically by Castryck and Decru: a single desktop core recovered the lowest-tier secret key in ~10 minutes. It proved that a fresh-looking hard problem can fall to one paper — a main reason hybrid schemes pair classical and PQ keys today. (IACR ePrint 2022/975, 30 Jul 2022)",
  "fx.rainbow": "Rainbow (multivariate signature, NIST Round 3 finalist) was broken by Beullens: an ordinary laptop recovered a lowest-tier private key in ~53 hours (one weekend). (IACR ePrint 2022/214, Feb 2022)",
  "fx.chen": "In April 2024, Yilei Chen posted a claimed polynomial-time quantum algorithm for LWE; on 18 April the author noted an unfixable bug in Step 9. Lattice foundations survived the test, illustrating that \"no known quantum attack\" is constantly being stress-tested. (IACR ePrint 2024/555)",
  "fx.records": "Classical factoring records fell twice in September 2026: RSA-260 (862 bits, 3 Sep, Eric Lu using a GPU CADO-NFS port written with Devin, ~4,900 GPU-days) and RSA-896 (896 bits, 19 Sep, Stephen Weis porting CADO-NFS to up to 2,048 GPUs with Claude, ~30 GPU-years). Checked: both published factor pairs multiply back to the RSA challenge numbers. The largest public prime-field discrete log record located remains 795 bits from 2019. (Cognition blog; saweis.net; IACR ePrint 2020/697)",
  "fx.qdemo": "Public quantum factoring demos still factor only integers tiny enough to split by hand. In a tongue-in-cheek paper, Gutmann and Neuhaus \"reproduced\" those records using a 1981 VIC-20, an abacus, and a dog to highlight the gap. (IACR ePrint 2025/1237, Jul 2025)",
  "fx.qrsa": "Craig Gidney (Google Quantum AI) estimated that under 0.1% gate error and 1 µs surface-code cycles, fewer than 1 million noisy physical qubits could factor 2048-bit RSA in under a week — down from his 2019 estimate with Ekerå of 20 million qubits in 8 hours. This is a resource estimate, not a demonstration. (arXiv:2505.15917, 21 May 2025)",
  "fx.qecc": "Babbush, Gidney, Boneh et al. (Google Quantum AI & collaborators) estimated that 256-bit elliptic-curve discrete logs require fewer than 1,200 logical qubits and 90 million Toffoli gates — under 500,000 physical qubits in minutes on a 0.1%-error superconducting architecture. They withheld the circuit, using a zero-knowledge proof to certify its existence. (arXiv:2603.28846, 30 Mar 2026)",
  "fx.survey": "Global Risk Institute survey of 26 experts by Mosca and Piani: aggregated likelihood of a cryptographically relevant quantum computer within 10 years is 28%–49%, and 51%–70% within 15 years. Subjective expert polling; both authors are affiliated with quantum-security firm evolutionQ. (GRI, 9 Mar 2026)",
  "ch10.merit.title": "Figures of merit: key sizes, ciphertexts, and speeds",
  "ch10.merit.p": "Public-key algorithms compete on three numbers: security bits (Chapter 7), bytes transmitted, and speed. Post-quantum schemes are generally larger, yet ML-KEM actually runs faster than X25519; SLH-DSA carries large, slow signatures, but relies purely on hash-function security as the most conservative fallback.",
  "ch10.merit.cap": "Byte sizes: ML-KEM from FIPS 203 formulas, others cross-checked against liboqs datasheets. Speeds from Cloudflare's \"State of the Post-Quantum Internet\" (5 Mar 2024), explicitly marked rough and platform-dependent. Key-exchange speed is client ops/s; signature speed is sign/verify time relative to Ed25519.",
  "merit.th.alg": "Algorithm",
  "merit.th.pk": "Public key (B)",
  "merit.th.out": "Ciphertext / sig (B)",
  "merit.th.speed": "Speed",
  "merit.x25519": "X25519 (elliptic-curve key exchange)",
  "merit.mlkem512": "ML-KEM-512",
  "merit.mlkem768": "ML-KEM-768",
  "merit.mlkem1024": "ML-KEM-1024",
  "merit.ed25519": "Ed25519 (elliptic-curve signature)",
  "merit.rsa2048": "RSA-2048 signature",
  "merit.mldsa44": "ML-DSA-44",
  "merit.fndsa512": "FN-DSA-512 (Falcon)",
  "merit.slhdsa128s": "SLH-DSA-128s",
  "merit.slhdsa128f": "SLH-DSA-128f",
  "merit.ops": "{v} ops/s",
  "merit.rel": "sign {s}×, verify {v}×",
  "ch10.open.title": "Unresolved questions",
  "ch10.open1": "<strong>Exact hardness of lattice problems?</strong> Security margins rest on how lattice reduction behaves in hundreds of dimensions; whether margins are generous enough remains active research, as Chen's 2024 paper reminded everyone.",
  "ch10.open2": "<strong>Signature bloat.</strong> ML-DSA signatures start at 2,420 bytes, and a single TLS handshake carries several signatures, swelling certificate chains. Round-3 signature candidates and designs such as Merkle Tree Certificates aim to shrink them; Chrome still lists PQ signatures as \"planned\" (Cloudflare dev docs).",
  "ch10.open3": "<strong>Hybrid or pure post-quantum?</strong> NIST IR 8547 notes hybrids hedge against a single-algorithm break (as happened to SIKE) while adding complexity, cost, and bug surface, calling them transitional; IETF standardised hybrids and browsers ship hybrids today. Both views have weight and practice is still split.",
  "ch10.open4": "<strong>When will capable quantum computers arrive?</strong> Nobody knows. Resource estimates dropped by over an order of magnitude in a few years (from 20 million to fewer than 1 million qubits), while public quantum factoring demos remain at hand-calculable sizes. This page makes no prediction.",
  "ch10.beyond": "<strong>Two adjacent directions not modelled here.</strong> <strong>Zero-knowledge proofs</strong> let you prove \"I know a secret\" or \"this computation is valid\" without revealing the secret itself — the Google paper above used one to prove a quantum circuit exists without publishing it. <strong>Fully homomorphic encryption</strong> lets a server compute directly on ciphertexts so decrypting returns the computed result; most constructions also rely on lattice LWE and are still far slower than plaintext execution. Both lie outside this essay's scope.",

  /* ───── 11 ───── */
  "ch11.title": "Four Questions for Reading Cryptography News",
  "ch11.law": "Every headline announcing \"cipher broken\" or \"quantum breakthrough\" can be dissected with four quick checks.",
  "ch11.p1": "With the ladder behind you, you already hold the toolkit. Next time you read a news story, paper abstract, or vendor announcement, ask:",
  "ch11.q1": "<strong>Which hard problem does it rest on?</strong> Factoring, discrete logs (mod p or elliptic curve), lattice LWE, hashing, error-correcting codes… news against one family only touches schemes built on that problem.",
  "ch11.q2": "<strong>How many bits of security were lost?</strong> \"1,000× faster\" shaves off only ~10 bits — dropping from 128 to 118 bits still holds. Going from exponential/sub-exponential to polynomial time is the qualitative break (Chapters 7–8).",
  "ch11.q3": "<strong>Did the maths fall, or did one implementation leak?</strong> SIKE fell mathematically; many real breaches instead leak timing, reuse nonces, or skip padding (Chapter 4). Implementation bugs get patched without replacing the algorithm.",
  "ch11.q4": "<strong>Is it a demonstration, an estimate, or a forecast?</strong> For \"factored\", ask how many bits; \"qubits required\" is an estimate; \"which year\" is a forecast — check who made it and what stake they hold (Chapter 10).",
  "ch11.sum": "Two strangers agree on a secret in public through a door that is <em>easy forward and hard backwards</em>; they verify who is speaking through <em>signatures and a pre-installed list of roots</em>; and the strength of the door is measured in <em>bits of the best known attack</em>. Quantum computers break two of those doors, while lattices and hashes build replacements — already shipping in major browsers in 2026 as the old doors queue for retirement in the 2030s.",
  "ch11.links": "Related pages: <a href=\"../rsa-encryption/\">RSA Public-Key Encryption · Easy to Multiply, Hard to Split</a> · <a href=\"../../algorithms/hashing/\">Hashing · Squeezing Any Input Into a Fixed Fingerprint</a> · <a href=\"../../../math/arithmetic/number-combinatorics/\">Number Theory & Counting · Compute the Structure</a> · <a href=\"../../../physics/modern/quantum-basics/\">Quantum Basics · Possibility Before Outcome</a> · <a href=\"../../quantum/what-quantum-computers-do/\">What Quantum Computers Can Actually Do · From First Principles to the Frontier</a> · <a href=\"../../../tech/networks/how-a-web-page-arrives/\">How a Web Page Reaches Your Phone</a>",
  "ch11.warn": "One final reminder: the code on this page exists solely to illuminate the mechanisms. It uses predictable randomness, toy parameters, no padding, and no side-channel hardening — never use it to protect real data. For real encryption, use audited libraries (your language's standard library, OpenSSL, or libsodium) with their recommended defaults.",

  "footer": "A Secret Agreed in Public · <a href=\"../../../\">visual.integ.life</a><br>Models in this essay: square-and-multiply (Knuth TAOCP vol. 2 §4.6.3); baby-step giant-step (Shanks 1971; Handbook of Applied Cryptography Alg. 3.56); Diffie–Hellman (1976); RSA (1978) and e = 3 cube-root attack; elliptic-curve group law and Hasse bound (Silverman, The Arithmetic of Elliptic Curves, ch. III & V); SHA-256 (FIPS 180-4) and hash-then-sign; heuristic GNFS cost L<sub>n</sub>[1/3, (64/9)<sup>1/3</sup>] (o(1) omitted) and Pollard ρ; Shor's classical reduction (Nielsen & Chuang ch. 5); Mosca's inequality; Babai rounding (1986); Regev LWE encryption (2005) — all on toy teaching parameters.<br>Sources: NIST SP 800-57 Pt 1 Rev 5, FIPS 203/204/205, NIST IR 8547 (2026), NIST IR 8610, NIST HQC release (2025); IETF RFC 10024, RFC 8446, RFC 8017, RFC 7919, draft-cfrg-schwabe-kyber-04; Cloudflare blog (Mar 2024, Sep 2026) and dev docs; Google Security Blog; Signal and Apple security blogs; OpenSSH 10.0 release notes; Microsoft Security Blog; UK NCSC; European Commission; IACR ePrint 2020/697, 2022/214, 2022/975, 2024/555, 2025/1237; arXiv 2505.15917, 2603.28846; Cognition blog & saweis.net (Sep 2026); Global Risk Institute (2026); liboqs datasheets. Full links and access dates in this directory's BRIEF.md."
};
